    <item>
      <title>Telescope Spots Two Black Holes on the Cusp of Merging</title>
      <link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/12/science/space/james-webb-telescope-black-holes-merging.html</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/12/science/space/james-webb-telescope-black-holes-merging.html</guid>
      <atom:link href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/08/12/science/space/james-webb-telescope-black-holes-merging.html" rel="standout"></atom:link>
      <description>New images from the James Webb telescope show a galaxy with a trio of supermassive black holes. Two of them appear to be locked in a tight tango.</description>
      <dc:creator>Amanda Schupak</dc:creator>
      <pubDate>Wed, 12 Aug 2026 15:56:50 +0000</pubDate>
      <category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/des">Black Holes (Space)</category>
      <category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/des">James Webb Space Telescope</category>
      <category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_org">Astronomy and Astrophysics (Journal)</category>
      <media:content height="1801" medium="image" url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/08/12/multimedia/12SCI-MERGING-BLACKHOLES-01-mgfw/12SCI-MERGING-BLACKHOLES-01-mgfw-mediumSquareAt3X.jpg" width="1800"></media:content>
      <media:credit>Chris Gunn/NASA</media:credit>
      <media:description>Engineers working with the James Webb Space Telescope’s near-infrared spectrograph. The instrument can tease apart the individual wavelengths of light hitting it, which allowed scientists to observe gases behaving in ways only a black hole’s pull can cause.</media:description>
    </item>
